# A. V. Goldberg, S. A. Plotkin and E. Tardos, generalized circulation (1991)

Bibliography label: Goldberg--Plotkin--Tardos 1991.

Published source: A. V. Goldberg, S. A. Plotkin and E. Tardos,
*Combinatorial algorithms for the generalized circulation problem*,
Mathematics of Operations Research 16(2) (1991), 351--381.
DOI: `10.1287/moor.16.2.351`.

Generalized flow assigns a multiplicative gain to each directed arc: an input
amount is multiplied when it reaches the head. Products of gains therefore
govern cycles. This is the established network-flow setting adjacent to the
multiplicative-slope and Farkas layers of directed transport. Max-affine floors
and vertex-typed noninvertible maps are additional structure, not ordinary
generalized-flow data.

This file is a source-positioning note. It states no Lean version of the
paper's algorithms or complexity results.
